---
/* PHASE 4B — the survey queue, opened to anybody.
 *
 * 316 of 519 sources in this directory have never been opened. They are here
 * because a national register or an open dataset named them, which is worth
 * knowing and is not the same as somebody having looked. 199 have been
 * triaged — alive or dead, a page or a JavaScript application, open or
 * refusing robots — which turns a list of URLs into a queue.
 *
 * A QUEUE ONE PERSON WORKS IS A BOTTLENECK, and it is the clearest structural
 * risk this atlas has: the gap grows faster than one researcher can close it.
 * Surveying a source is ten minutes of work — open it, find whether the images
 * are free, and write down the sentence that proves it. That is something a
 * stranger with an evening can do, and there was no way for them to do it.
 *
 * WHAT THIS PAGE IS NOT. It is not a form that writes to the atlas. This is a
 * static site with no server and no account, and inventing one to accept
 * unverified edits would put the directory's one asset — that a row can be
 * trusted — in the hands of anybody with a browser. So it hands somebody a
 * specific source, tells them exactly what to look for, and gives them a
 * prepared message to send back. A person still reads it before it lands.
 *
 * The queue is ordered by what makes a survey cheap: a source whose homepage
 * we have already read, in a country where somebody is likely to read the
 * language, with the phrases we found already printed so the reader knows
 * what they are walking into.
 */
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
import { u } from "../lib/url.js";
import provs from "../../public/providers.json";
import { atlas as idx } from "../lib/atlas.js";
import "../styles/kit.css";

const CN = idx.countries || {};
const P = provs.providers;
const unsurveyed = P.filter((p) => p.access === "unsurveyed" && p.url);

/* READ FIRST, because those are the ones somebody can settle in ten minutes.
   `needs-browser` and the dead ones are real work or no work at all. */
const RANK = { read: 0, "needs-browser": 1, http: 2, unreachable: 3, declined: 4 };
const queue = unsurveyed.slice().sort((a, b) =>
  (RANK[a.triage] ?? 9) - (RANK[b.triage] ?? 9) ||
  String(a.name).localeCompare(String(b.name)));

const byCountry = {};
for (const p of unsurveyed) {
  for (const c of (p.countries || [])) (byCountry[c] = byCountry[c] || []).push(p);
}
const countries = Object.entries(byCountry)
  .map(([cc, list]) => ({ cc, n: list.length, name: CN[cc] || cc }))
  .sort((a, b) => String(a.name).localeCompare(String(b.name)));  // by name: a list ordered by size reads as unsorted

const readable = queue.filter((p) => p.triage === "read").length;
const surveyed = P.filter((p) => p.access && p.access !== "unsurveyed").length;
---
<Base
  title="Survey a source"
  eyebrow="Ten minutes, one honest sentence"
  dek="Open one archive's website, find out what it actually costs to look, and send back the sentence that proves it. This is the work that turns a grey dot into an answer.">

  <p class="k-stats" aria-label="Summary">
    <span><b>{unsurveyed.length}</b> sources nobody has opened</span>
    <span class="k-tag is-new">{readable} easy starts</span>
    <span class="k-tag">{surveyed} already done</span>
  </p>

  <div class="k-hi">
    <p>Nine places in ten on this map have never been surveyed. Not because the records are missing, but
    because <b>nobody has opened the archive's website and written down what is behind it</b>.</p>
    <p>That one fact is the difference between a researcher spending an evening well and spending it on a
    catalogue card, and finding it out takes about ten minutes per source.</p>
  </div>

  <h2 class="k-h">How it goes</h2>
  <ol class="k-steps">
    <li><b>Open the site.</b> One of the {readable} below whose homepage we have already read is the
      easiest start.</li>
    <li><b>Find the records, not the homepage.</b> Look for the register viewer. In French it is usually
      <em>archives en ligne</em> or <em>état civil</em>; in Italian <em>registri</em>; in German
      <em>Kirchenbücher</em>.</li>
    <li><b>Answer one question: what does it cost to look?</b>
      <ul>
        <li class="k-tag is-new">free · images, no account</li>
        <li class="k-tag">account · register first</li>
        <li class="k-tag">index · names, no images</li>
        <li class="k-tag">catalogue · a card, unphotographed</li>
        <li class="k-tag">onsite · in the building only</li>
        <li class="k-tag">paid or mixed · a charge</li>
      </ul>
    </li>
    <li><b>Copy the sentence that proves it</b> and the page it is on. Not a summary: the site's own words.
      That quotation is what lets the next person check you instead of trusting you.</li>
    <li><b>Send it.</b> <a href={u("/add/")}>Through this form</a>, with the source's name, the class,
      the quotation and the URL.</li>
  </ol>

  <h2 class="k-h">Two rules, and they matter more than the speed</h2>
  <ul class="k-tiles two">
    <li><span class="k-tag">Stop</span><span><b>If a site refuses robots or asks you not to, stop.</b> Record that it declined and why. This
      project does not defeat bot gates or pretend to be a browser it is not: an atlas that asks archives to
      trust it cannot be the thing that ignores them.</span></li>
    <li><span class="k-tag">Say so</span><span><b>If you cannot tell, say you cannot tell.</b> An honest <em>unsurveyed</em> is worth more than a
      confident guess. A place wrongly marked <em>free</em> costs somebody an evening and costs this
      directory the only thing it sells.</span></li>
  </ul>

  <h2 class="k-h" style="margin-top:34px">Pick a country <span class="k-count">{countries.length}</span></h2>
  <p class="k-lede">Language is the real barrier, so start where you can read.</p>
  <ul class="cc">
    {countries.map((c) => (
      <li><a class="k-tag" href={`#cc-${c.cc}`}>{c.name}<span>{c.n}</span></a></li>
    ))}
  </ul>

  <h2 class="k-h" style="margin-top:34px">The queue</h2>
  <p class="k-lede">Ordered by how cheap each one is to settle. A source whose homepage we have already read
  is a ten-minute job; one that needs a browser to render is longer; one that did not answer may simply be
  gone.</p>

  <table class="k-table sv-q">
    <thead><tr>
      <th class="q1">Source</th><th class="q2">Where</th><th class="q3">What we know</th>
    </tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      {queue.slice(0, 220).map((p) => (
        <tr id={`cc-${(p.countries || [])[0] || "zz"}`}>
          <td><a href={p.url} target="_blank" rel="noopener">{p.name}</a></td>
          <td class="where">{(p.countries || []).map((c) => CN[c] || c).join(", ")}</td>
          <td class="sv-note">{p.triageNote || "not yet opened by anybody"}</td>
        </tr>
      ))}
    </tbody>
  </table>
  {queue.length > 220 && (
    <p class="cap">and {queue.length - 220} more. The whole list is in
    {" "}<a href={u("/providers.json")}>providers.json</a>, where
    <code>access: "unsurveyed"</code> is the queue.</p>
  )}

  <aside class="k-foot">
    <p>Stuck on a language, or not sure what you are looking at?</p>
    <p>Send what you have through <a href={u("/add/")}>the form</a>. A half-answer with a link beats a silence.</p>
  </aside>
</Base>

<style>
  .k-steps ul li { text-transform: none; letter-spacing: 0; font-family: var(--sans); font-size: 12px; margin: 0; }
  .two { grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fit, minmax(280px, 1fr)); max-width:var(--measure); }
  .two li { gap: 10px; }
  .cc { list-style: none; padding: 0; margin: 8px 0 0; display: flex; flex-wrap: wrap; gap: 8px; }
  .cc a { text-decoration: none; color: var(--ink-2); font-family: var(--sans); text-transform: none;
          letter-spacing: 0; font-size: 12.5px; }
  .cc a:hover { border-color: var(--accent); color: var(--accent); }
  .cc a span { color: var(--ink-3); margin-left: 6px; font-family: var(--mono); font-size: 11px; }
  .sv-q { table-layout: fixed; }
  .q1 { width: 34%; } .q2 { width: 18%; } .q3 { width: 48%; }
  @media (max-width: 760px) { .sv-q { table-layout: auto; } .q1, .q2, .q3 { width: auto; } }
  .where { white-space: normal; font-family: var(--sans); font-size: 12.5px; }
  .sv-note { font-family: var(--mono); font-size: 11.5px; color: var(--ink-3); }
  .sv-q tr { scroll-margin-top: 80px; }
</style>
